<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraham Fischer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abraham Fischer was a politician of the Orange Free State and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a>. He served on the Free State executive and as one of its envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. When responsible government was granted in 1907 he became prime minister at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orangia-unie">Orangia Unie</a> party, and after 1910 he was a minister in the first Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> cabinet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-fischer.jpg" alt="Abraham Fischer" width="234" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atelier Straugh, formerly Kameke, Javastraat 20, The Hague, The Netherlands Original uploader was Doortmont at en.wikipedia … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abraham_Fischer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fischer was born on 9 April 1850 in Green Point, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, to Johannes Jacobus George Fischer, formerly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, and Catherina Anna Margertha Brink. He was educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-cape-town">South African College</a> and became a lawyer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, joining the bar in 1875. In 1873 he married Ana Robertson, the daughter of Scottish immigrants to the Free State.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Drawn into the politics of the Orange Free State, Fischer became a member of its Volksraad in 1878, its vice-president in 1893 and a member of the executive council in 1896, and he took part in many colonial and interstate conferences. During the Boer War he headed a joint deputation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and the Orange Free State to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and America to seek support for the Boers. He returned in 1903 to practise law in the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Still promoting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> cause, Fischer helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orangia-unie">Orangia Unie</a> party in May 1906 and became its chairman. The party won a majority of seats in the colony's first elections in November 1907, and on 27 November he was chosen prime minister, the only man to hold the office. He served until the colony ceased to exist with the union of 31 May 1910.</p><p>He then joined the cabinet of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> as Minister of Lands, was made a Privy Councillor in 1911 and became Minister of the Interior and Lands in 1912. He died on 16 November 1913. His son Percy Fischer became Judge President of the Orange Free State, and his grandson was Bram Fischer, the anti-apartheid activist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abraham Fischer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Liberal victory in Britain, responsible government was granted in 1907, and the Orangia Unie party under <b>Abraham Fischer</b> took office.</blockquote><a href="/state/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Green Point, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.9,18.4&amp;year=1890" aria-label="Green Point on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5019 3393 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abraham Fischer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5469 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5469 3663h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.9,18.4&amp;year=1890">What was here in 1890? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
